# SHOTGUN — Devin Desktop Project Rules

## What this project is
Shotgun is an Australian road-alert and navigation app. It fuses six data
sources (crowd API, state police mobile-camera notices, fixed camera/speed
limit data, a Facebook-derived AI agent, in-app user reports, and roadkill/
breakdown hotspots) into one confidence-scored map, with two modes:
Cruising (passive/ambient) and Navigate (turn-by-turn with fastest/safest/
side-streets-only routing).

## Stack (do not deviate without asking)
- Mobile app: Expo / React Native, TypeScript
- Maps & routing: Mapbox GL + Mapbox Directions API
- Backend: Node.js / TypeScript on Vercel serverless functions in
  /server in front of Neon Postgres — extend this, don't scaffold a
  second backend, and never introduce an always-on service
- Database: Postgres + PostGIS as a Neon extension (spatial queries are
  mandatory for corridor-relevance and route-avoidance — never
  approximate with plain lat/lng math)
- Live delivery: no websocket/persistent-connection layer. Cruising Mode
  alerts go out via Expo push notifications; the foreground live map
  short-polls GET /alerts/nearby every 15–30s
- Cache: Upstash Redis (REST-based, serverless-native) in front of the
  corridor-relevance query so polling doesn't hammer Postgres — cache
  layer only, NOT a pub/sub broadcast transport
- Ingestion: queue-based via Upstash QStash (HTTP-based, triggers Vercel
  functions), never synchronous scraping inline with API requests
- Scheduled jobs: GitHub Actions cron, matching the existing
  refresh-waze-alerts.yml / refresh-fixed-cameras.yml pattern — no
  node-cron, no worker process, and no Vercel Cron (the repo has no
  linked Vercel project, and GHA scheduling already works here)
- Billing: RevenueCat
- Error tracking: Sentry, wired in from the first commit
- Dev/staging infra: Neon branch databases for dev (pairs with Vercel
  preview deployments) + Upstash free tier for Redis/QStash — no local
  Docker; point at the real services from day one

## Dev environment
- The repo-root `.env` is the PROD reference. Nothing in dev reads it.
  Verification runs, tests, migrations, and any ad-hoc script must use the
  `DATABASE_URL` in `server/.env`, which points at a Neon *branch*.
- Ask before running anything against a connection string you can't
  identify as a branch — a DATABASE_URL copied from root `.env` is prod,
  and "it's only an additive change" is not a reason to run it there.

## Non-negotiables
- Every alert, regardless of source, MUST conform to the alert schema
  in /shared/alert-schema.ts before it touches the database. No source
  writes its own shape directly to the alerts table.
- No sponsor, partner, or paid placement may ever influence route
  calculation, alert confidence scoring, or which alerts surface.
  Sponsorship is presentation-layer only.
- The alert schema never stores identifying details of individuals
  (no names, plates, or personal identifiers) — location and incident
  type only.
- Nightly scraper jobs must fail loudly (Sentry alert), never silently.
- All Facebook-derived alerts require human-in-the-loop review until
  explicitly told otherwise per group.

## Code conventions
- TypeScript strict mode, no `any` without a comment explaining why
- One file per source integration under /server/sources/
- Exception to the above: scheduled source scrapers (police notices,
  fixed cameras, dataset loads) live in /scripts/ as plain CommonJS JS,
  with shared machinery in /scripts/scrapers/lib/ and one file per
  state under /scripts/scrapers/states/. They are NOT TypeScript and
  NOT under server/ because GitHub Actions runs them as
  `node scripts/x.js` with only the root pnpm install available -
  server/ is a separate npm project (own package-lock, not in the pnpm
  workspace) and the workflow pattern has no TS build/run step. Keep
  new scrapers dependency-free (Node built-ins + root devDeps only).
- All spatial queries go through /server/lib/postgis-helpers.ts,
  never raw inline SQL scattered across services
- Tests live next to the file they test (*.test.ts, colocated
  __tests__/ directories)

## UI rules (enforced, not aspirational — see checklist below)
- Every screen imports spacing, colour and type values from
  /src/theme/tokens.ts. No hardcoded hex codes, no hardcoded pixel
  values for padding/margin/gap anywhere in a screen or component file.
- Absolute positioning (`position: 'absolute'`) is banned outside of
  /src/components/MapOverlayPanel.tsx. If a screen "needs" absolute
  positioning to stop things overlapping, that's a sign it should be
  using a layout primitive (flex row/column with gap) instead — fix
  the layout, don't patch it with positioning.
- Every screen is built from the base component set in
  /src/components/base/ (Card, AlertPill, BottomSheet, MapOverlayPanel,
  ScreenContainer). No screen defines its own one-off card, pill, or
  sheet styling — extend or compose the base components.
- ScreenContainer enforces safe-area insets and a consistent outer
  padding (tokens.spacing.screenPadding) — every screen is wrapped in
  it, no exceptions.
- Maximum 3 levels of nested View before a layout component (Row/Column/
  Stack from the base set) must be used instead of raw Views.
